+# Betterlife BF63112 (SPI)
+
+_STATUS: stub.  This page documents a driver that has been built but has not
+yet run against hardware._
+
+## Device
+
+The BF63112 is a match-in-sensor fingerprint module from Betterlife.  On the
+ONEXPLAYER 3 it sits on the Intel LPSS SPI bus as `ACPI\BF63112`
+(`spi-BF63112:00`); there is no USB interface.  The module keeps its own
+templates, so a Linux driver stores only opaque handles.
+
+The firmware describes the bus parameters in the `_CRS` resources of the
+`FPRT` ACPI device: SPI clock, mode, word size, chip select, the interrupt
+line and a reset line.  None of those values are hard-coded in the driver.
+On a normal boot `spidev` is not bound to the device.  The udev rule that
+libfprint generates from the driver's id table does that binding; no static
+rule file is installed by this series.
+
+## Transport
+
+A transaction is a one-byte selector followed by a payload, or by a
+receive-only clock:
+
+    selector = (opcode & 0x3f) << 1            host reads
+    selector = 0x80 | ((opcode & 0x3f) << 1)   host writes
+
+Frames are:
+
+    AA | L:u32 LE | 16 x 0 | 4C op phase 00 arg:u16 len:u32 data | pad16 | CRC-32
+
+`L = 20 + align16 (10 + data_len)` counts the bytes after the start-of-frame
+byte, without the CRC.  The CRC is CRC-32/ISO-HDLC over every preceding byte,
+including the `0xAA`.  A query without data is 41 bytes with `L = 36`.  The
+answer uses the same layout; the opcode is at offset 22, the phase at 23, the
+body length at 27 and the body at 31.  The driver validates the CRC and the
+opcode.
+
+## Supported operations
+
+| opcode | meaning | driver use |
+|---|---|---|
+| `0xA0` | firmware version | informational, at open |
+| `0xB6` | sleep | not sent; suspend closes the spidev node |
+| `0xC1` | sensor information | required, at open and after resume |
+
+Enrol, verify and identify return `FP_DEVICE_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED` on purpose.
+Their payloads, the template handle and the score encoding are not decoded,
+and the driver must never guess at biometric state.  No opcode that can
+destroy a template is on the driver's read allowlist.
+
+The `0xA2` handshake (SM2 ECDH, GM/T 0003 KDF, SM3 tag, SM4-wrapped key) is
+not needed for the read-only queries and is not part of this driver yet.
+
+## Remaining unknowns
+
+* No hardware transcript exists.  Every statement about the device above is
+  derived from static analysis of the vendor Windows driver or from the
+  module's own framing rules, not from an observed exchange on Linux.
+* The SPI clock, mode, word size and GPIO polarity come from `_CRS`; the
+  values on this machine have not been read (the table is root-only).
+* The sensor information and firmware field layouts are inferred from the
+  handler's reads and have not been confirmed against a live answer.
+* The interrupt line is not exposed by the SPI controller driver, so the
+  driver falls back to a timed poll.  The optional sysfs/libgpiod paths are
+  compile-tested only.
+* Whether the module echoes the request phase in the answer is unverified;
+  the driver checks the opcode only.
+* Chunks of the `0xB4` image command can exceed the default `spidev` buffer
+  (4096 bytes); the command is not implemented.
+
+## Bring-up
+
+The driver takes optional environment configuration while the ACPI values are
+not available to it: `BF63112_SPIDEV`, `BF63112_SPI_SPEED`, `BF63112_SPI_MODE`,
+`BF63112_SPI_BITS`, `BF63112_CRS`, `BF63112_IRQ_GPIO` and the reset-line
+variables.  A real submission should replace these with a proper device
+property or `_CRS` lookup; they exist so a first hardware session can happen
+without changing the driver.
+
+See the top of `drivers/bf63112.c` for the complete list and the safety rules.
